{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkSc9JiboHXrnCufN86d","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The completeness check now exists: **124 of 124 requirements from `PLAN.md` and TASK.md hold on the shipped build**, and the table is at `docs/PLAN-CHECK.md` in the dev folder. Each row has its own evidence: 50 are tests that passed on this run, 73 are values read from the game data, the built file or the running page, and 1 is a file on disk.\n\nThe checker compares real values, including:\n- each op's title, surgeon, arena, time, vitals, drain and doses against the plan's table\n- the plan's starting balance numbers and difficulties\n- menu, Settings, Pause and Dev Mode items, read from the running page\n- all head tags, and `pixel-engine.js` byte-for-byte against the released kit\n- the session client against the hub's current copy\n- the exit-interview fields, and the About screen against `exit-interview.json` on all 14 answers\n\nIts first run reported 2 failures, both a wrong pattern in my checker for the host-leave and server hub scenarios. The suite had run and passed both, so nothing in the game needed fixing.\n\n## Results on the shipped build\nThe shipped file's hash is `ca43919793d3b2dd`, and no game code changed this round.\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `node tests/run.mjs`, every suite in one pass | 647 passed, 0 failed |\n| Requirement check | 124 of 124 |\n| `check.mjs` on the page, `--headless` with keys, and `--sheets` | all pass (last run on this same build, in the previous pass) |\n| Extra hub runs | 5 of 5, five more times, with no `earlyoom` kills during them |\n\n## Limits\n- **What the table can't show:** it checks what a script can read. Whether something feels right is a judgement, and no person has played the game. TASK.md says a human or a real controller is \"never required\".\n- **The old hub crash:** its cause stays unconfirmed, because its error and process are gone. It hasn't recurred in any later run, and no test failed in it.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-05 09:28:50\n- **Source:** Trauma Center: Second Opinion (Atlus, Wii, 2006)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** surgical action with visual-novel medical drama; first-person surgeon's-eye close-up on a 3D operating field, with talking-head close-ups and 3/4 room shots in story scenes\n- **Tech:** Pixel Engine 0.46.0 (riftlight kit: three.js r186, Rapier, WebGPU), own systems in JavaScript, esbuild 0.28.2, AI Hub session client 1.4.0\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native low-poly 3D rendered as pixel art; procedural painted tissue canvases, original busts, tools, strains and sets; Kenney furniture recoloured for generic props\n- **Animation:** the engine's native rig clips on custom figures, SpringChain, LegStepper and Squash, plus procedural bust, tool, heartbeat and breathing animation\n- **Audio:** everything synthesised in code: engine Song chiptune music (10% default), SoundDef effects, Animalese-style gibberish voices from pure-JS formant syllables\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world, 1 to 4 seats; couch co-op on its own with no network\n- **Testing:** headless tools allowed, unlimited: Node sim, content and real-stage replay tests, the kit `check.mjs`, Playwright Chromium browser tests with stub pads, a bot playthrough of the whole game, five hub harness runs, screenshots at 1080p. No person played it\n- **Research:** the plan's planner read the source's Wikipedia page, the hub's protocol and the engine kit, and ran engine spikes; during the build I read the kit's guides and the hub README, and did no further research on the source\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** added on import\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** About 29 in the game: 8 from scripts (balance report, sim tests, the sound lint, which found a misnamed wave making nine sounds and the choirs fail, the kit replay check, which found level swaps racing a replay, and a pad test failing under load, which led to one press acting twice or not at all when the frame rate dipped), about 13 from screenshots, including a Dies Irae crown that never drew. Reading the code against the plan line by line then found 8 more that no test had caught, such as sliders that did not reach a running op and a missing wheel for co-op seats. Two more failures were bugs in the tests themselves.\n- **How many would be critical or game breaking?** 1, now fixed: Op 4 could not be finished, because two bone fragments hid under pools that were already gone. The balance report found it, and the bot now clears Op 4 at every skill above idle.\n- **Most helpful test:** the whole game played by the bot in the real page on manual time: every scene, op and screen with the view running, in about a minute.\n- **Least helpful test:** the rumble check: a stub pad only proves the call path runs, and no script can say how it feels. No person played this game, so feel and real-player difficulty are untested.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most about the engine?** Models as plain data plus the pixel pipeline. A 3D bust from a few boxes and slabs looks like a shipped game, with no art files.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Rarely. `wasPressed` misses taps when a test presses and releases inside one slow software frame, so tests had to hold keys across frames. `check.mjs --headless` replays only the last level and never yields, so my async level swaps failed it until I deferred them. Palette-only model colours forced a material swap for skin tones.\n- **Most important thing you had to build yourself?** The painted tissue canvas: a per-frame 480x270 texture on a displaced mesh, which carries pools, wounds, stitches, gel and guides. Next, per-pad input with analog triggers.\n- **Hardest part about using the engine?** Slow software WebGPU made browser tests timing-sensitive. Manual stepping with `engine.step` solved most of it.\n- **What did you change in the engine, and why?** none\n- **If you could change one thing?** Per-pad gamepad state with analog triggers built into Input, instead of one merged pad, so couch co-op needs no polling of its own.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **What did you like most about this prompt?** The plan marks what was verified and gives working numbers, so I could trust them and spend the effort on the game.\n- **What was confusing?** Very little. The title riff asks for the source name while the plan keeps every other use of it out, so I chose a title that riffs on the second opinion without repeating the name.\n- **What was the hardest thing to do?** Judging hands-on feel and difficulty with no person playing: the bot proves each op can be cleared, not that it feels right.\n- **What would you change in this prompt?** Say how long a first-time human should take per op, so a bot-based balance report can be checked against something.\n\nNothing is committed to git.\n\nFiles are in `work/`:\n- `SecondStitch-TraumaCenterSecondOpinion-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261105-092850.html`\n- `exit-interview.json`\n- `PROGRESS.md`\n- `SecondStitch-TraumaCenterSecondOpinion-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261105-092850/` (dev folder, with `docs/PLAN-CHECK.md` and screenshots in `shots/`)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3159,"cache_read_input_tokens":650736,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3159},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9464247a-cdba-4289-905c-cd8b34e5d270","uuid":"5d04126d-cfa0-4fe3-9c90-db08538d338e","timestamp":"2026-11-05T20:51:33.601Z","request_id":"req_011CgkSc8yN4bm7Rrf9oKxcY"}
